WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.659 --> 00:00:04.660
This is an abortion rights demonstration in
1972.

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:07.249
People marched to demand the right to legal
abortions.

00:00:07.249 --> 00:00:12.809
And the next year, the Supreme Court case
Roe v Wade granted them that right.

00:00:12.809 --> 00:00:17.470
But fast forward almost 50 years, and protests
for abortion rights are still happening.

00:00:17.470 --> 00:00:19.390
“What do we want?

00:00:19.390 --> 00:00:20.390
Access!“

00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:24.640
A lot of the fight over abortion policy centers
on restrictive laws set by state governments.

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:28.050
And the courts that uphold or strike down
those laws.

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:29.480
Including the Supreme Court.

00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:33.120
But the President and Congress
hold a unique power over abortion access,

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:37.460
because they have the final say on the federal
dollars that support it.

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:42.480
In every federal budget for the past 43 years,
among all the programs the government funds

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:47.170
— like the military, foreign aid, and education
— there’s also language about something

00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:49.820
the government can’t spend federal money
on:

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:51.489
“coverage of abortion….

00:00:51.489 --> 00:00:55.280
Except in the case of “rape or incest”
or if the pregnancy would “place the woman

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:57.530
in danger of death.”

00:00:57.530 --> 00:00:59.610
This provision is called the Hyde Amendment.

00:00:59.610 --> 00:01:03.750
And it disproportionately affects low-income
people who rely on federally-funded health

00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:05.360
care.

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:10.040
Every president since 1976 has supported the
Hyde Amendment by approving an annual federal

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:12.650
budget from Congress that included it.

00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:17.700
But the 2020 election could change this, because
while one candidate supports the Hyde Amendment,

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:25.530
the other has vowed to oppose it.

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:27.680
The Hyde Amendment’s introduction into the
federal budget

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:32.460
Can be traced back to Roe V. Wade,
In the years immediately following this decision,

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:35.960
the federal government paid for abortions
through Medicaid, which accounted for roughly

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:38.520
a third of all abortion procedures.

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:42.700
But the anti-abortion backlash was swift—
Including in Congress.

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:48.310
In 1976, Illinois Representative Henry Hyde
proposed the abortion-resticting Hyde Amendment

00:01:48.310 --> 00:01:50.420
during an annual budget hearing.

00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:56.390
It passed with a 199-165 vote, ending up in
that year’s spending bill where it has stayed

00:01:56.390 --> 00:02:00.371
ever since
voted for by anti-abortion politicians, and

00:02:00.371 --> 00:02:04.670
by pro-abortion-rights politicians
And, in every case, these budgets have been

00:02:04.670 --> 00:02:07.240
approved and signed by the sitting president.

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:12.340
In 1977, Henry Hyde made the intentions of
his amendment clear:

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:16.280
He said “I certainly would like to prevent,
if I could legally, anybody having an abortion,

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:20.480
a rich woman, a middle-class woman, or a poor
woman,” he said.

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:24.570
“Unfortunately, the only vehicle available
is the … Medicaid bill.”

00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:29.340
There’s a handful of agencies directly affected
by the Hyde Amendment that provide health

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:33.920
care through federal funding, like the Indian
Health Service and Peace Corps.

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:35.260
Medicaid is the biggest, though.

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:40.160
It pays for basic health services for just
over 76 million people in the US who live

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:43.290
near the poverty line or are disabled.

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:46.750
Part of Medicaid’s funding comes from federal
dollars — the other part comes from the

00:02:46.750 --> 00:02:47.920
state you live in.

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:53.120
Abortion care in the United States is largely
driven by where you live… both in terms

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:59.020
of the availability of services and in addition
to that, the ability of insurance to pay for

00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:02.220
it
These 16 states will help Medicaid users cover

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:03.990
the cost of abortion.

00:03:03.990 --> 00:03:08.190
But these 34 states and Washington D.C. won’t.

00:03:08.190 --> 00:03:11.810
Anyone seeking an abortion there has to pay
the full bill.

00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:16.730
That means nearly 8 million people of child-bearing
age who live in these states aren’t covered

00:03:16.730 --> 00:03:20.440
for abortion services because of the Hyde
Amendment.

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:23.730
These Medicaid restrictions place the biggest
burden on low-income people.

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:28.840
Laurie Roberts [00:10:16] We could basically
quit doing the work we do.

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:31.000
If the Hyde Amendment didn't exist.

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:34.980
Laurie Roberts manages an abortion fund in
Alabama, one of the most restrictive states

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:36.180
when it comes to abortion access.

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:41.120
So an abortion fund is a organization that
helps people access abortion care.

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:45.819
It can be anything that removes the barriers
to getting to and from the clinic.

00:03:45.819 --> 00:03:49.920
And then there's just direct financial support
for the abortion procedure.

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:53.970
The price of an abortion in the first 10 weeks
of a pregnancy, without coverage, is roughly

00:03:53.970 --> 00:03:57.569
$500, and it gets more expensive in later
weeks of pregnancy.

00:03:57.569 --> 00:04:01.110
That’s a lot of money for someone who qualifies
for Medicaid.

00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:06.430
To be eligible, you have to make less than
138% of the federal poverty line.

00:04:06.430 --> 00:04:10.400
For a single person, that’s less than an
$18,000 annual income.

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:13.970
For a family of 2, it’s $24,000 dollars.

00:04:13.970 --> 00:04:16.790
And for a family of 3, it’s about $30,000
dollars.

00:04:16.790 --> 00:04:21.459
And because of racial disparities in our country,
Medicaid recipients are more likely to be

00:04:21.459 --> 00:04:22.540
Black or Hispanic.

00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:28.810
I can personally say what it was like for
me...I went to the clinic…

00:04:28.810 --> 00:04:33.410
I wanted to have a procedure.

00:04:33.410 --> 00:04:38.970
And I couldn't, I didn't have the money in
time..That doesn't mean that I don't love

00:04:38.970 --> 00:04:46.570
my child...But what it means is I went through
a lot of emotional and physical trauma because

00:04:46.570 --> 00:04:49.870
what should have been a decision that was
only mine was taken away from me.

00:04:49.870 --> 00:04:57.430
A study of 269 women from 2015-2017 in Louisiana,
a state that doesn’t cover abortion through

00:04:57.430 --> 00:05:02.470
Medicaid, shows that 29% of women would have
had an abortion had Medicaid covered it.

00:05:02.470 --> 00:05:10.440
Which is the intended effect for anti-abortion
advocates: fewer people having abortions.

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:13.410
Abortion restrictions like the Hyde Amendment
have made their way into other types of health

00:05:13.410 --> 00:05:15.030
insurance coverage, too.

00:05:15.030 --> 00:05:19.350
In 2010, Congress didn’t pass the Affordable
Care Act until it included language saying

00:05:19.350 --> 00:05:22.080
marketplace plans don’t have to cover abortion.

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:26.220
Today, these states won’t cover it under
marketplace plans.

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:30.520
And these states even restrict private insurance
companies from covering the procedure.

00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:35.320
And so that more and more people are affected
and they may not even know that they've been

00:05:35.320 --> 00:05:38.460
affected until they seek abortion care

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:43.220
“But it’s the medicaid restrictions — both
federal and state — that specifically target

00:05:43.220 --> 00:05:44.760
poor people.”

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:46.540
abortion is like many other services.

00:05:46.540 --> 00:05:52.260
If you have means, you can either have insurance
that pays for a service or you can afford

00:05:52.260 --> 00:05:54.480
to pay for these services out of pocket.

00:05:54.480 --> 00:06:01.010
And if it's not offered in a place that's
close to you, you can afford to either travel,

00:06:01.010 --> 00:06:05.430
get lodging, take time off or get childcare.

00:06:05.430 --> 00:06:09.990
All of those things are things that are available
to higher income women that may not be available

00:06:09.990 --> 00:06:12.260
to lower income women.

00:06:12.260 --> 00:06:16.460
The two candidates in the 2020 election are
solidly in their partisan corners on the issue

00:06:16.460 --> 00:06:17.930
of abortion access.

00:06:17.930 --> 00:06:20.020
But that wasn’t always the case.

00:06:20.020 --> 00:06:25.300
Joe Biden took office in the Senate in 1973,
the same year Roe V. Wade passed.

00:06:25.300 --> 00:06:29.930
And he's supported the Hyde Amendment since
it entered the federal spending bill in 1976.

00:06:29.930 --> 00:06:35.020
But in 2020, this is the landscape: where
the state you live in and how much money you

00:06:35.020 --> 00:06:39.180
have are the biggest factors in whether someone
can access an abortion.

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:42.860
Which is why Joe Biden, as a presidential
candidate, says he changed his stance.

00:06:42.860 --> 00:06:47.700
"If I believe heath care is a right, as I
do, I can no longer support an amendment that

00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:52.370
makes that right dependent on someone's zip
code,"

00:06:52.370 --> 00:06:55.130
And Trump has only dug deeper in his anti-abortion
position.

00:06:55.130 --> 00:07:01.930
"I notified Congress that I would veto any
legislation that weakens pro-life policies

00:07:01.930 --> 00:07:11.680
It’s also why the stakes for abortion policy
for the 2020 election are clear.

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:16.290
It’s a choice between a President who plans
to lift this barrier to abortion access for

00:07:16.290 --> 00:07:18.889
the country’s most vulnerable people.

00:07:18.889 --> 00:08:11.680
And one who will keep the status quo, at their
expense.

